$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM General/Typical Procedure: 2.2 Baylis-Hillman reaction involving
acrylamide and various aldehydes Procedure using condition set A: A 1 mL
reaction vessel equipped with a stirring bar was charged with acrylamide
(142 mg, 2.0 mmol), DABCO (224 mg, 2.0 mmol) and phenol (47 mg, 0.5
mmol). Note: If the aldehyde component is a solid it is added at this
stage. The vessel was fitted with a septum, gently flushed with Ar gas
and fitted with an Ar balloon. A tBuOH/H2O solvent mixture (120 μL) and
the aldehyde (3.0 mmol) were then added via syringe. The resulting
homogeneous mixture was stirred at 55 °C for the time indicated in Table
3 and allowed to cool. Column chromatography of the reaction mixture
(95:5 CH2Cl2/MeOH) yielded the required Baylis-Hillman adducts.
2-[Hydroxy(phenyl)methyl]acrylamide (4), yield 70% Spectral data
consistent with that reported in the literature3 M.p. 97-99 °C; IR (film)
3331, 3212, 3031, 1665, 1626, 1594 cm-1; 1H NMR (400 MHz, DMSO-d6) δ 5.49
(d, 1H, J= 4.5 Hz), 5.60 (s, 1H), 5.69 (d, 1H, J= 4.5 Hz), 5.79 (s, 1H),
6.98 (s,1H), 7.22 (m, 1H), 7.30 (m, 4H), 7.45 (s, 1H); 13C NMR (100 MHz,
DMSO-d6) δ 71.1, 117.4, 126.7, 126.9, 127.9, 143.3, 147.5, 168.7; HRMS
(EI) m/z calcd for C10H11NO2 177.0790, found 177.0788; Anal. Calcd for
C10H11NO2 C, 67.78; H, 6.26; N, 7.90. Found C, 67.29; H, 6.23; N, 7.77.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM regioselective, Baylis-Hillman reaction
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Acrylamide in the Baylis-Hillman Reaction: Expanded reaction scope and
the unexpected superiority of DABCO over more basic tertiary amine
catalysts
